Types of Data

Data is mainly classified into two categories:

1. Quantitative Data (Numerical Data)

Quantitative data consists of numerical values.

(a) Discrete Data

Discrete data contains countable whole numbers.

Examples:

Number of bank accounts
Number of children in a family
Number of students
(b) Continuous Data

Continuous data can take any numerical value within a range.

Examples:

Weight
Height
Temperature
Speed
Marks
2. Qualitative Data (Categorical Data)

Qualitative data describes categories or qualities.

(a) Nominal Data

Nominal data represents categories without any ranking.

Examples:

Gender
Blood Group
PIN Code
City Name
(b) Ordinal Data

Ordinal data represents categories with a meaningful order or ranking.

Examples:

Customer Feedback:
Poor
Average
Good
Excellent
Performance Rating
Education Level
Scales of Measurement of Data

The four scales of measurement are:

Nominal Scale – Categories without ranking.
Ordinal Scale – Categories with ranking.
Interval Scale – Equal intervals but no true zero (e.g., Temperature in °C).
Ratio Scale – Equal intervals with a true zero (e.g., Height, Weight, Age).

